<h2>Trust Boundaries</h2>
<p>
  FluxEncoder executes FFmpeg and FFprobe on media files, directory trees, and remote URLs that may originate from untrusted sources. Its security model separates trusted operator intent from untrusted media and manifest inputs:
</p>

{{ signalPipeline({
  title: "TRUST BOUNDARY & ISOLATION TOPOLOGY",
  stages: [
    {
      step: "TRUSTED",
      owner: "Operator & Host",
      name: "CLI Flags, settings.json & FFmpeg Binary",
      detail: "Direct CLI options, administrator-owned settings.json, and the local FFmpeg installation.",
      tag: "Trusted Control Plane"
    },
    {
      step: "ISOLATION",
      owner: "FluxEncoder",
      name: "ArgumentList & Protocol Whitelist",
      detail: "Zero shell invocation; absolute path normalization; per-input -protocol_whitelist; secret redaction.",
      tag: "ProcessStartInfo.ArgumentList"
    },
    {
      step: "UNTRUSTED",
      owner: "External Inputs",
      name: "Media, Metadata, Playlists & Fluxfiles",
      detail: "Filenames, container tags, network HLS/concat playlists, remote servers, and repository Fluxfiles.",
      tag: "Sanitized & Constrained"
    }
  ]
}) }}

<ul>
  <li><strong>No shell invocation:</strong> Every FFmpeg and FFprobe argument is passed as an individual token in <code>ProcessStartInfo.ArgumentList</code>. Shell metacharacters in filenames or metadata are never interpreted by <code>/bin/sh</code> or <code>cmd.exe</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Untrusted media & metadata:</strong> File and directory names, container tags, subtitle tracks, playlists, and FFmpeg stderr diagnostics are treated as untrusted data.</li>
  <li><strong>Untrusted Fluxfiles:</strong> Because <code>Fluxfile.yaml</code> manifests are shared across repositories, <code>flux apply</code> blocks raw FFmpeg flags, custom binary paths, YAML anchors/tags, and non-<code>FLUX_*</code> environment variables.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Threats & Mitigations</h2>

<h3>Hostile Filenames</h3>

