<h3>Hostile Metadata & Network Playlists</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Per-input protocol whitelists:</strong> A malicious network playlist (such as HLS/M3U8 or <code>concat</code>) could reference local <code>file:</code> paths. FluxEncoder attaches an explicit <code>-protocol_whitelist</code> matched to each input's scheme:
    <ul>
      <li>Local file input: <code>file,crypto,data</code></li>
      <li>HTTPS input: <code>http,https,tcp,tls,crypto,httpproxy</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Metadata privacy stripping:</strong> Camera and phone recordings frequently embed GPS coordinates, device serial numbers, and owner names. FluxEncoder strips private location and device tags from outputs unless <code>--keep-private-metadata</code> is passed, hides them in <code>flux info</code> unless <code>--show-private</code>, and drops <code>data</code> streams (which can carry GPS telemetry tracks) unless <code>--keep-data</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Remote Sources & Credential Protection</h3>
<ul>
  <li><code>remote://ALIAS/path</code> definitions in <code>settings.json</code> store only the scheme, host, port, username, and base path. Any URL containing an inline password in <code>settings.json</code> is refused.</li>
  <li>Secrets are stored exclusively in the operating system's credential store (Windows Credential Manager, macOS Keychain, or Linux Secret Service via standard input) or read from <code>FLUXENCODER_SECRET_REMOTE_&lt;ALIAS&gt;</code> environment variables injected by a secret manager.</li>
  <li>URI resolution percent-encodes the username, password, and each path segment while rejecting <code>.</code> and <code>..</code> traversal segments.</li>
  <li><code>SecretRedactor</code> masks URL userinfo passwords, secret query parameters (<code>token</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>sig</code>, <code>signature</code>, <code>pass</code>), RTMP stream keys, <code>Authorization</code> headers, and every registered secret (both raw and percent-encoded) across terminal output, JSON reports, SQLite history, and crash diagnostics.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Output Integrity & Atomic Staging</h3>
<ul>
  <li>FFmpeg writes to a hidden temporary file (<code>.&lt;name&gt;.flux-&lt;id&gt;.partial</code>) in the destination directory. Only outputs that exist, are non-empty, and stay within configured size limits are atomically moved to the final path.</li>
  <li>A job stopped by FluxEncoder (cancellation, stall timeout, or runtime limit) is never marked successful even if FFmpeg exits <code>0</code> after receiving <code>q</code>.</li>
  <li>Incomplete <code>.partial</code> files from failed or cancelled runs are deleted automatically (or retained under their <code>.partial</code> suffix when <code>--keep-partial</code> is requested).</li>
</ul>

