<h2>Raw FFmpeg Arguments & Managed Environments</h2>
<p>
  <code>--ffmpeg-arg</code> passes raw options directly to FFmpeg. Because raw FFmpeg flags can read or write arbitrary files or open network connections, <code>--ffmpeg-arg</code> bypasses source policies and folder containment. FluxEncoder logs an explicit warning whenever it is used.
</p>
<p>
  In multi-tenant or service deployments, administrators can lock down execution by setting <code>FLUXENCODER_MANAGED=1</code> in the service environment and providing a read-only <code>settings.json</code>:
</p>

{{ terminalFrame({
  title: "/etc/fluxencoder/config/settings.json — managed service policy",
  stream: "JSONC Policy",
  code: "// Owned by root/administrator, read-only for the service account
// Run with FLUXENCODER_HOME=/etc/fluxencoder and FLUXENCODER_MANAGED=1
{
  \"security\": {
    \"allowExtraArgs\": false,
    \"allowPipes\": false,
    \"allowDevices\": false,
    \"allowedSchemes\": [\"https\"],
    \"allowedRoots\": [\"/srv/media\"]
  },
  \"limits\": { \"maxRunMinutes\": 240, \"maxInputMB\": 20000, \"maxOutputMB\": 20000, \"threads\": 8 },
  \"history\": { \"retentionDays\": 30, \"maxRuns\": 5000 }
}"
}) }}

<h2>Residual Risks</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Process argument visibility:</strong> When a password-authenticated URL is passed to FFmpeg, local OS users on the same machine may inspect process arguments unless <code>/proc</code> is mounted with <code>hidepid=2</code> (Linux). Prefer SSH key-based SFTP authentication or short-lived pre-signed URLs in shared hosts.</li>
  <li><strong>Unstructured secret strings:</strong> Redaction masks registered credentials and known URL/header/query patterns; a secret pasted into a plain filename cannot be distinguished from ordinary text.</li>
  <li><strong>FFmpeg codec parser vulnerabilities:</strong> Memory-safety bugs inside FFmpeg's native demuxers or decoders are outside FluxEncoder's process boundary. Keep FFmpeg patched and run service workloads inside unprivileged containers or sandboxes.</li>
</ul>
